A refining module now checks that an inherited class member still meets the contract of the trait member it overrides, whenever the module changes either member or gives the class a new parent. Before, a refining module could strengthen a trait member's postcondition, or refine an abstract type into a class that extends a trait, while it inherited the overriding member unchanged, and the override check against the new contract was never made: a call through the trait then proved `false`. Pairs that a refining module inherits unchanged are not checked again. The hidden option `--legacy-elided-override-checks` restores the old, unsound rule, for comparison and migration only.
